WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.510
Narrator: Warner Bros. released

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:06.400
the first trailer for "Joker,"

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:09.520
an origin story about
Batman's most famous foe,

00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:11.510
starring Joaquin Phoenix.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:13.560
There's a lot of references to movies

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.150
and the original comic books alike.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:18.150
Here's everything you might have missed.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:27.320
In the first shot, we see Arthur
Fleck, not in clown makeup,

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.430
meeting with a social
worker named Debra Kane.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.277
Kane was also featured in the comic

00:00:32.277 --> 00:00:34.460
"Batman: The Ultimate Evil."

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:36.300
Bruce Wayne uses her as an informant

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:38.830
to investigate a pedophile ring.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:42.370
We see Fleck sitting in a room
working on a sheet of jokes.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:44.150
As we see later in the trailer,

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:46.620
this Joker is an aspiring comedian.

00:00:46.620 --> 00:00:48.935
In the Batman story "The Killing Joke,"

00:00:48.935 --> 00:00:50.780
The Joker is also portrayed

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:53.531
as a struggling stand-up comedian.

00:00:53.531 --> 00:00:55.381
The Joker: I got nervous
and blew a punchline.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:00:58.620
Narrator: If you take a
close look at the notebook,

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:00.250
you can see that The Joker's material

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:01.930
pulls from his real life.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:04.070
There's references to mental illness,

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:06.290
insomnia, and poverty.

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:08.900
It's important to note that
this is a comic book movie

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.900
heavily inspired not just
by the source material,

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.290
but also by a lot of movies.

00:01:14.290 --> 00:01:16.340
The filmmakers have stated
that their intention

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.760
was not just to make a DC movie

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:20.350
but rather one that looks a lot like

00:01:20.350 --> 00:01:22.110
a Martin Scorsese movie.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.990
The movie is set in Gotham in 1981,

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:26.450
and the look of it seems pulled from

00:01:26.450 --> 00:01:30.070
the 1970s and 1980s New
York, as you can see

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:34.500
from the shots of the street,
the cars, and the subway.

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:38.620
Martin Scorsese's 1976
masterpiece, "Taxi Driver,"

00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:40.770
also takes place in this setting.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.940
In the movie, Robert De Niro plays

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:46.740
insomnia-stricken Vietnam
war veteran Travis Bickle,

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.450
who rides the city streets at night

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:51.910
and plunges into unimaginable darkness.

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:55.681
In one of the movie's most
famous lines, Bickle says,

00:01:55.681 --> 00:01:57.793
Travis Bickle: There's no escape.

00:01:57.793 --> 00:01:59.580
I am God's lonely man.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:01.340
Narrator: Warner Bros. described "Joker"

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:04.840
as an exploration of a
man disregarded by society

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:06.960
that is not only a character study

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:09.570
but also a broader cautionary tale.

00:02:09.570 --> 00:02:10.930
This scene with Arthur on a date

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.340
is also reminiscent of
Travis Bickle's scenes

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.930
with Cybill Shepherd before he melts down.

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.540
The other Scorsese movie
this pulls heavily from

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:21.370
is 1982's "The King of Comedy."

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:24.860
In that dark comedy, De
Niro plays Rupert Pupkin,

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:27.010
a man so obsessed with becoming a comedian

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:29.590
that he kidnaps his
favorite talk show host

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:32.320
in order to finally get himself on TV.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.340
In "King of Comedy," Pupkin
lives with his mother,

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.410
and in "Joker," it appears
Fleck does as well.

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:41.100
His mother is played by Frances Conroy,

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:44.780
who also appeared in the
2004 movie "Catwoman."

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.070
Later in the trailer, we see The Joker

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:48.790
greet a TV audience on a set

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:52.060
that looks a lot like the one
from "The King of Comedy,"

00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:54.160
and the host of that
show appears to be played

00:02:54.160 --> 00:02:57.060
by none other than Robert De Niro.

00:02:57.060 --> 00:02:59.200
It also appears that
The Joker is inspiring

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:02.240
a lot of rabid, violent followers.

00:03:02.240 --> 00:03:04.140
The twist at the end
of "The King of Comedy"

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.990
is that, despite Pupkin landing in prison,

00:03:06.990 --> 00:03:10.360
he becomes a beloved,
if infamous, celebrity.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:13.860
The movie references go
all the way back to 1936.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.820
We see Fleck getting
kicked out of a screening

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:19.400
of the silent Charlie Chaplin
classic "Modern Times"

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:21.660
at a building called Wayne Hall.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.470
The trailer is also partially scored

00:03:23.470 --> 00:03:26.137
by a remix of the Chaplin song "Smile."

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:32.540
At one point we hear him say,

00:03:32.540 --> 00:03:37.140
The Joker: My mother
always tells me to smile.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:38.190
Narrator: And let's not forget

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:40.180
one of The Joker's most famous lines

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:41.617
from "The Dark Knight."

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:45.570
The Joker: Let's put a smile on that face.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:47.780
Narrator: At one point,
we see Arkham Asylum,

00:03:47.780 --> 00:03:50.680
which plays an integral role
in the "Batman" universe,

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:53.140
particularly with its villains.

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:56.120
At one point, we see
The Joker greet a child.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.860
This appears to be a young Bruce Wayne.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:00.300
Could this be leading to The Joker

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.300
murdering Wayne's parents?

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:04.230
The murder of Bruce's parents pops up

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.300
in almost every "Batman" movie.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.490
In the 1989 "Batman," in which

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:10.460
Jack Nicholson plays The Joker,

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:12.930
The Joker is the one
who commits the crime.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:15.650
This isn't always the
case, but we're wondering

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:18.100
if that's what this movie is leading to.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:21.030
Similarly, Arthur's attacked in an alley

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:23.570
just as Bruce Wayne's parents were.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:25.790
Thomas Wayne, Bruce Wayne's dad,

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:28.610
even appears briefly in this shot on TV.

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:30.240
The Joker's makeup towards the end

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:32.730
is similar to the mask
worn by the protesters

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.270
earlier in the trailer,

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:36.060
and he is seen dyeing his hair green,

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:39.160
just like the wig he wears
while working in the streets.

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:41.170
Oh, and we finally get to hear

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:43.353
this new Joker's trademark laugh.

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:50.270
Narrator: Did you notice
anything we didn't?

00:04:50.270 --> 00:04:51.853
Let us know in the comments.

